backend/alembic_app_schema — Postgres port of the app's primary schema
=========================================================================

Migrations for the app's primary-datastore tables (currently SQLite,
backend/jobs.db) ported to Postgres's default `public` schema, on the same
shared Supabase-capable Postgres engine as `linkedin.jobs`
(backend/core/postgres.py). See backend/alembic_app_schema/versions/
413f4ed8fbc6_create_app_tables.py's docstring for the full schema-audit
and type-mapping rationale (SQLite TEXT/VARCHAR/JSON/BOOLEAN -> Postgres
TEXT/VARCHAR/JSONB/BOOLEAN, one intentionally-unenforced FK).

IMPORTANT — schema-only, not a cutover
----------------------------------------
Running this migration creates empty tables in Postgres. It does NOT change
what the running app reads from or writes to — that is still SQLite
(backend/core/database.py's ENGINE, see CLAUDE.md) until a real cutover
(repository rewrite + dual-write/backfill/verify/switch plan) is separately
scoped and executed. Treat this as schema/tooling readiness, not a live
migration.

Every table here is hand-authored DDL generated once from an exact audit of
backend/jobs.db (PRAGMA table_info/foreign_key_list/index_list), not
reflected from ORM model classes — 13 of the app's 19 tables are managed by
raw-SQL repository modules with no declarative model at all (see
backend/repositories/*.py), so there's no metadata to autogenerate against.
Any future schema change must be hand-added as a new revision here, mirrored
from whatever change was made to backend/jobs.db's schema
(backend/core/migrations.py or a repository's own DDL).

Run every command from the repository root, with `backend/.venv` active.

How the target database is chosen
------------------------------------
Same as backend/alembic_linkedin — `alembic_app_schema.ini`'s
`sqlalchemy.url` placeholder is always overwritten at runtime
(backend/alembic_app_schema/env.py) with `backend.config.DATABASE_URL`:

  1. `DATABASE_URL`               — always wins; this repo uses this one
  2. `DATABASE_URL_DEV`/`_PROD`   — picked by `APP_ENV` (only if the bare
                                    key is unset; a genuine two-project split)
  3. `DB_HOST_DEV`/`_PROD` + friends — built from discrete parts

`env.py` prints `[alembic_app_schema] APP_ENV=... — migrating <host>`
before every run. Read the **host**, not the APP_ENV: with the bare
`DATABASE_URL` set (form 1, what this repo uses) the target is fixed and
`APP_ENV` changes nothing, so the two can disagree.

Running migrations (single project — what this repo uses)
-----------------------------------------------------------
    alembic -c alembic_app_schema.ini upgrade head

Running migrations against a separate Dev project, if one exists
------------------------------------------------------------------
Requires the bare `DATABASE_URL` to be unset and `DATABASE_URL_DEV` set:

    APP_ENV=development alembic -c alembic_app_schema.ini upgrade head

Running migrations against Prod
----------------------------------
Review first (`--sql` prints the DDL without executing/connecting):

    APP_ENV=production alembic -c alembic_app_schema.ini upgrade head --sql
    APP_ENV=production alembic -c alembic_app_schema.ini upgrade head

Checking current revision
----------------------------
    APP_ENV=development alembic -c alembic_app_schema.ini current
    alembic -c alembic_app_schema.ini history

After the schema exists: data copy
--------------------------------------
See backend/scripts/migrate_jobs_db_to_supabase.py — copies backend/jobs.db's
rows into these tables in FK-safe order, resets Postgres identity sequences
for the 5 autoincrement-PK tables (chat_sessions, confidence_audit_log,
job_feedback, match_triggers, shadow_match_scores) to MAX(id)+1 afterward,
and runs row-count + spot-check verification. Same two-step safety gate as
backend/scripts/linkedin_israel_jobs.py: requires both
`--allow-write` and `ALLOW_SUPABASE_APP_MIGRATION=true` before touching
Postgres; `--dry-run` (the default) only reads SQLite and reports what
would be copied.
